92186045417] [17592186415849 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "6dc55032-e087-5612-84b6-a80c771dff9e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415850 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186415794 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415850 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186415795 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415850 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186415798 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415850 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186415805 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415850 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186415811 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415850 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415850 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415850 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186415795 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415850 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "8258478b-1136-5626-9c52-3604e6dc2cb6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415858 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186415800 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415858 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186415815 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415858 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186415822 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415858 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186415827 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415858 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186415833 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415858 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415858 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415858 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186415827 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415858 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "edcf7bc8-f757-5f28-b389-f3fbae1ad417" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415860 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415847 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415860 #edb/ref 1069 "q-109288" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415860 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415860 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the triangle $C$ in the plane with vertices $(-1,0),(1,0)$ and $(-1,2),$ and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ Express ${∮}_{C}({y}^{2}-2xy)\\,dx+(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-{x}^{2})\\,dy$ as a repeated integral." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415860 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0fd75856-9255-5679-88e6-6aeda24c9a6c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415860 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/ea/eac7a442f0a794db0606b95aa722320d099f2bcc4e530fc6b62e9be889335da2.png)\n\n \n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={y}^{2}-2xy$ and $Q(x,y)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-{x}^{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2y-2x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1-x\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({y}^{2}-2xy)\\,dx+(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-{x}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-2x-(2y-2x)]\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-2y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415862 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415850 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415862 #edb/ref 1069 "q-109196" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415862 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415862 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the rectangle $C$ in the plane with vertices $(0,1),(0,-1),(1,-1)$ and $(1,1),$ and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ According to Green's theorem, \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,dx-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\,dy={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nCalculate the function $f(x,y).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415862 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "25b5bd80-ef51-5481-b2fb-7d3f90359e46" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415862 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/a3/a3e81cfacc47dfa241a5fea64de84e375a58a6d86ceab82c86303763d5e41fe5.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,dx-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}[-2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-(-2y)]\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2y(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy))\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x,y)=2y(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415864 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415858 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415864 #edb/ref 1069 "q-109199" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415864 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415864 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the triangle $C$ in the plane with vertices $(-1,0),(0,-1)$ and $(1,1),$ and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ According to Green's theorem, \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2xy)\\,dx+2{sin}^{2}⁡(xy)\\,dy={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nCalculate the function $f(x,y).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415864 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4af8cfd2-bdac-5f91-b83d-a441b5fa79ce" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415864 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/f4/f4bb6636c104cf17bcd4b25037a3490ba30b7c1380b152c33504d9015903bdca.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2xy)$ and $Q(x,y)=2{sin}^{2}⁡xy.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2xy),\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=4y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)=2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2xy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2xy)\\,dx+2{sin}^{2}⁡(xy)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2xy)-(-2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2xy)))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2xy)+2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2xy))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2(x+y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2xy)\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x,y)=2(x+y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2xy).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415869 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415849 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415869 #edb/ref 1069 "q-109144" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415869 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415869 #edb/ref 1071 "Use Green's theorem to evaluate ${∮}_{C}(2xy-{y}^{2})\\,dx+({x}^{2}+2x)\\,dy,$ where $C$ is the boundary of the triangle with vertices at $(-1,0),$ $(1,0)$ and $(-1,2).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415869 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8c2f5a35-b3df-565f-bebb-8a3475dfd0d6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415869 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/cb/cb85e51584f0c41fc19210a5ce56289baa652aff3046d5d88c034d187b7e45dd.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=2xy-{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{2}+2x.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2x-2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =2x+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1-x\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(2xy-{y}^{2})\\,dx+({x}^{2}+2x)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x+2-2x+2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2+2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}(2+2y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{[2y+{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{1-x}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(2(1-x)+(1-x)^{2})dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{2}-4x+3)\\,dx \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}-2{x}^{2}+3x]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{3}-2+3]-[-\\frac{1}{3}-2-3] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}+6 \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186428635 #edb/ref 1030 0.5 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428635 #edb/ref 1032 "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account multivariable: 1 rises / 2 eligible passed source activities = 0.500000; nearest selected fraction = 1/2. Crossing activity numbers [2]; waits [2]. Separate account estimates: multivariable=1/2 (1/2). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: True. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacademy/encompassing-analysis-2026-10-11/frequency-fractions/multivariable-frequency-estimates.json, pair 2116->2152. All account evidence retained in the prepared import's selected-relationships.json." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428635 #edb/ref 1036 #edb/ref 17592186050264 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186537083 #edb/ref 1168 "generate/scratch/17592186133882.py" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186537083 #edb/ref 1169 #uuid "fd0b4ebc-434e-5be0-ad61-971d93a9c7c0" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186537268 #edb/ref 1080 "y-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537268 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537268 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "143eeb18-acb6-58b9-9d2a-0e8621cf072a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537350 #edb/ref 1080 "y" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537350 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537350 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3afaeacd-5ea5-5eab-9723-41fa0b9df340" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537478 #edb/ref 1080 "x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537478 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537478 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "805ee25c-bc46-5a2b-bb0b-a7850c3300e2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537533 #edb/ref 1080 "-y" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537533 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537533 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a17a715e-bff4-5662-80e2-9bdfe2c285ec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537742 #edb/ref 1080 "x" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537742 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537742 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f92e502d-c078-59ac-9d95-4be2a12cda17" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537833 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186537268 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537833 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186537350 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537833 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186537478 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537833 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186537533 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537833 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186537742 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537833 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537833 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537833 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186537350 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537833 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "a172fe47-3553-5b48-9c9c-d02e78168ac3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537906 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537833 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537906 #edb/ref 1069 "q-109131" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537906 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537906 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the triangle $C$ in the plane with vertices $(0,0),(1,1)$ and $(0,1),$ and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ According to Green's theorem, \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,dx+(xy+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,dy={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nCalculate the function $f(x,y).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537906 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "40abd682-d4fa-55d0-8dea-db00bcc211a6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537906 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/56/5673a3eadfa3c01dbb9e2c8bf497b17f34a834d950621afa7d480af625ce1e18.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ and $Q(x,y)=xy+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=y-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,dx+(xy+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}[y-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)]\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(y-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x,y)=y.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
